/** Siman 092 translations — part 2: baer-heitev, kereti, pitchei-teshuva, rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd, nekudot-hakesef */
export const TRANSLATIONS_P2 = {
  'baer-heitev': {
    '1#א': `The gentile. See siman 98 s.k. 15 that we require specifically kafeilah or mislaleta.`,
    '1#ב': `That it imparts taste. The reason: we fear the milk itself absorbed in the meat and the piece became nevelah, went out and mixed in the rest of the milk, and cannot be tasted — for milk in milk it is; therefore one needs sixty in the milk against the whole piece, for we do not know how much was absorbed and discharged from it.`,
    '2#א': `The piece. Shach wrote the explanation is thus: when it fell on one piece, the rest in the pot do not join to nullify the milk but need sixty in the piece itself to nullify the taste; when there is not sixty in the piece the piece becomes nevelah; when he stirs afterward and the piece enters among the rest of the pot it forbids the rest in the pot until sixty against the piece — but if he stirred immediately at the time of falling the piece immediately enters the whole pot and everything joins and it is not forbidden unless there is not sixty in the pot and piece against the milk, and same with covering. That which Tur wrote siman 105 seif 4 — if wholly outside the gravy it is only kedei netilah for R' Yehuda, and for Rashi even partly in the gravy — perhaps milk differs as a fat issur, or it deals with a thick issur that does not spread except through gravy; on the first answer it is hard from Rashba and Ran that milk is not called a fat issur — see Taz siman 87 seif 10 who proved milk is a fat issur and left the matter b'tz'ar.`,
    '2#ב': `Sixty. Shach wrote: Tur brought the dispute of Rashi and R' Yehuda — R' Yehuda holds only when the whole piece is outside the gravy, e.g. the piece beneath has part outside the gravy and this one rests on it; but if part is in the gravy, boiling mixes the taste in the whole pot and everything joins to nullify the milk. Rashi explains even if part is in the gravy not everything joins to nullify the milk. The view of all authors except Rashba and R' Yerucham is like R' Yehuda, and so all Acharonim; Taz wrote there is a leniency for Rashi on one side — if part is in the gravy and this piece is forbidden it does not forbid the rest; Taz wrote it does not appear so to him, for even though the gravy below does not help nullify the drop of milk that fell since boiling does not reach there, nevertheless that piece itself is forbidden — the lower part from the upper part — and returns and forbids all the gravy and what is in it until sixty against the whole piece, for the piece becomes nevelah since part of the piece rests in the gravy it conveys to the rest. (These words are astonishing in my eyes, for if so what difference is there in Rashi's view between stirring or not, and so it appears from Shulchan Aruch since he rules and likewise if he did not stir at all, if there is sixty in the pot against the drop of milk that fell it forbids only the piece alone — therefore it deals when it is in the gravy as explained later; and per his words in Darkei Moshe it implies one needs sixty against the whole piece — b'tz'ar.) And Taz wrote further from Shulchan Aruch's words which are Rambam's words it appears he holds like Rashi; and even though Maharshal in his Orach Chaim ruled like R' Yehuda, in his book ch. 25 siman 49 he was stringent like Rashi. (And it is hard what stringency there is in Rashi's view more than R' Yehuda's, for even when there is sixty in the pot there is stringency for Rashi that for R' Yehuda if it is in the gravy everything joins to nullify, not so for Rashi; nevertheless when there is not sixty in the pot it is the reverse — for Rashi only that piece is forbidden and for R' Yehuda everything is forbidden — and one must say he holds in truth for both stringently: if there is sixty we go stringently like Rashi, and if there is not sixty also stringently like R' Yehuda — requires study.)`,
    '2#ג': `Permitted. Nevertheless all other pieces that touched this forbidden piece need netilah as siman 105 seif 7. Shach wrote: one might say when there is not sixty in the pot it should also be permitted since he did not stir at all and it only imparted taste in that piece; it appears what requires sixty in the pot is when part is in the gravy, for the Rav is in doubt whether we hold like R' Yehuda who mixes taste in all or like Rashi — therefore the pot is permitted either way and the piece is forbidden lest he does not mix taste in all and the pot does not join. (Beit Yosef emends it should read thus, and likewise if he did not stir at all neither at start nor end nor cover at all — if there is not sixty in the piece against the drop of milk that fell it forbids only the piece alone and the rest of the pot is permitted.)`,
    '2#ד': `And likewise. Shach wrote: even though it appears one can explain the Mechaber's words that he requires stirring specifically from beginning to end — and therefore Rama should have written in language of "some say" — nevertheless since the Mechaber's words can also be explained thus, as Mahari ibn Chabib explained Rambam's words, therefore he did not write in language of "some say"; but in truth the Mechaber's view is not so, etc. — see there.`,
    '2#ה': `Stir. Shach wrote: and one might say how can he stir the pot that everything mix — perhaps there is taste in one piece and how can one nullify issur l'chatchila; one may say since it is not known on which it fell the whole pot enters doubt and there is no issue of nullifying issur; Rama who is Tur's view holds since it is possible to establish that a gentile tastes each piece and piece, if so not everything enters doubt and stirring afterward does not help — see siman 105.`,
    '3#_': `Fit to be honored. And if it is not fit to be honored it is nullified in majority as below siman 109 — see there.`,
    '4#א': `Itself. Shach wrote: one might say below siman 106 he wrote the piece itself remains in its issur because the issur in it is not fully discharged, and the Rav in the gloss there understood these are contradictory and to avoid contradiction he explains thus in explanation: what he wrote here means per what he wrote earlier regarding basar b'chalav that even the gravy is permitted with sixty, nevertheless the piece remains in issur; if it is chatichah hefker and he does not recognize it it is not nullified in a thousand — and he teaches that it is not so in other issurim but nullified in majority when not recognized since there is no issur from the substance itself, as below siman 101; and then even the piece itself returns to being permitted and permitted to eat all of it and need not throw one away as below siman 109. The reason the Mechaber distinguishes basar b'chalav from other issurim is because in basar b'chalav there is no recognizable issur except after mixture — therefore the whole mixture is the substance of the issur forbidden by Torah "mixing meat in milk"; not so other things that are issur in themselves and that is the Torah issur, only here it mixed incidentally and you have only a Torah doubt and chatichah na'asah nevelah does not apply — so Maggid Mishneh.`,
    '4#ב': `The issurim. Shach wrote in name of poskim that nevertheless for all it is only rabbinic in other issurim (and I already wrote above siman 90 that even in rabbinic issur we say chatichah na'asah nevelah).`,
    '4#ג': `Attached. Taz wrote in name of Semak: since it is attached to this issur, and likewise a worm in its body, and likewise a kosher fish found in its innards after cooking with a sheretz or non-kosher fish and there was not sixty in the fish itself against it — in all such cases we fear perhaps once it remained alone in the gravy at the end of pouring, or perhaps once they took it out boiling outside the pot and it became nevelah and after returning it was not permitted and forbids the rest until sixty against it. From this it appears in a fish where a sheretz was found or a piece of sheretz in its head and cooked thus with other fish one needs sixty against the whole head of the fish, for one law and one reason with what is found in its innards; and further proof from Orach Chaim general rule 36 regarding an apple with a worm — attached issur requires sixty in cooking against the whole apple if the apple itself does not have sixty against the worm; and so I saw in Maharil, etc. — peel the place for its taste is nullified in sixty even in cooking if there is sixty in the piece where it was found; and so he ruled on a piece of carp found in the head of a cooked fish to nullify its taste if there is sixty in the head of the fish against it. However per the reasoning it does not seem attached, for it rests where gravy can come from all sides; and since chatichah na'asah nevelah in other issurim is only rabbinic as I wrote, one may lean to be lenient here too and it is not chatichah na'asah nevelah — see siman 72 seif 3 what Taz and Shach wrote there, that the reason for attached issur is because it absorbs faster than other meat.`,
    '4#ד': `The gravy. Shach wrote: it appears what the Rav wrote in basar b'chalav that even if part is in the gravy chatichah na'asah nevelah — this is when he stirred afterward until all entered the gravy, and not that we say from the start everything joins to nullify because part is in the gravy as in other issurim; he teaches in basar b'chalav it is not so. Regarding what he wrote in other issurim nevertheless to be stringent to forbid that piece — it appears this is the Rav's own reasoning, for he is in doubt whether we hold like Rashi or R' Yehuda; but regarding the rest of the pot he did not want to be stringent since otherwise some say we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah except in basar b'chalav; nevertheless it is already explained that the view of all authors and Acharonim is like R' Yehuda.`,
    '4#ה': `Fell. Taz wrote in name of Maharshal: this is specifically when mixed not in the manner of cooking; but if it is the manner of cooking through imparting taste — issur mixed there and afterward fell from the gravy into permitted food — one needs sixty against all the gravy that fell, not to consider how much of the issur was absorbed in proportion in this gravy; but wet with wet such as wine in wine or water in forbidden water we can say we do not say all the permitted became nevelah, especially since it is rabbinic, and if more permitted was added afterward it is permitted — so it appears from his words that even not in case of loss we do not say in wet that became nevelah contrary to Rama here.`,
    '4#ו': `In any. Meaning even in basar b'chalav we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah in dry — its law as below siman 109. Regarding whether we say chatichah na'asah nevelah in salting, Terumat HaDeshen wrote: even though they practice to estimate all salting at sixty, nevertheless since many poskim hold all salting only forbids kedei kezayit one may permit for great loss or for a poor person in an important matter and say it is not chatichah na'asah nevelah; and it appears there that even in a fatty matter that forbids until sixty as below siman 105 we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah — and so appears primary; see siman 22 whether we say chatichah na'asah nevelah in kelipah — see siman 134 regarding chatichah na'asah nevelah. Taz wrote what Rama wrote if dry mixed with dry means not in the manner of cooking together — then its law is the issur is nullified in majority; therefore if first mixed not in majority one need not afterward majority against the whole first mixture; but if they cooked together certainly we say chatichah na'asah nevelah and need sixty in permitted against the whole piece. He wrote further Tur for R' Tam's view that even in other issurim "possible to squeeze it out" forbids and chatichah na'asah nevelah specifically in noten ta'am when there was not sixty in permitted to nullify the issur absorbed in it, but if forbidden in mashahu even if forbidden due to its species forbidden in mashahu for R' Yehuda the whole does not become nevelah. The reason is from what is stated in ch. 25 — R' Tam's answer, etc. — and since we say this reasoning in mashahu in min b'mino for R' Yehuda which is from Torah, all the more we say thus in rabbinic mashahu such as chametz on Pesach and yayin nesech; certainly what was first forbidden due to its species in mashahu does not forbid afterward if it fell to others and there is sixty against it, for then mashahu does not forbid; and we do not say two mashahu. Tur wrote even if forbidden due to its species forbidden in mashahu for R' Yehuda — meaning from Torah as I explained; and all the more chametz on Pesach for us whether in its species or not at sixty, for if forbidden in mashahu it does not become nevelah if it would forbid afterward in mashahu — we do not say two mashahu. A dry piece forbidden in mashahu and not recognized remains in issur and has no permission except if recognized and removed, for its discharge does not forbid in mashahu since it was only forbidden first in mashahu — so Bach. (Nekudat HaKesef disagrees with Taz and wrote he asked all elder rabbis and all said with one mouth they never practiced to forbid all mixtures — see there many laws related to Pesach.)`,
    '5#א': `The drop. Shach wrote: this is to permit the food but the pot is nevertheless forbidden and he should pour the food immediately on another side — so Semak: a drop that fell on the vessel wall — we are in doubt in ch. Dam Chatat whether it steams through the whole vessel or not; therefore when it fell opposite the gravy and there is sixty in the gravy from the drop it is permitted either way — if it steams through the whole vessel it is nullified, and if it does not steam then it should not forbid, meaning if it does not steam through the whole vessel it should not forbid because presumably it steams inward and is as if it fell in the food. (But I have difficulty: per this reasoning why is the pot forbidden — we should also say either way; and likewise why is it obvious that if it steams through the whole pot certainly the drop is nullified — we do not know how much discharges from it; therefore it appears as I wrote.) Taz wrote: if there is sixty in the food and it fell opposite the food it is permitted either way — if it steams in all of it, since it has steaming it enters inward into the food and the food nullifies it; if it does not steam, meaning it does not steam at all but stands in its place, there is no issur at all since it does not spread from its place and does not enter inward — Semak did not use language "if it steams through the whole pot" only because it follows Talmudic language; but in truth Semak's primary intent is as I wrote — and per this the pot is also permitted either way; if it steams inward it is nullified in sixty, and if it does not steam it should not forbid the second food cooked in it from the first. (But if it fell opposite the empty place we fear it steams a large part of the pot until near the gravy and perhaps a very large part spreads so there is not sixty in the food against that part — therefore forbidden to pour through that place. In this part of the pot the issur remains forever and nullification does not help — therefore do not touch until it cools.) Therefore Maharshal ch. 25 wrote in this even the pot is permitted from law to cook meat again, only l'chatchila forbidden to use it even for meat that appears as if forbidden in milk.`,
    '5#ב': `Place. Meaning that part of the vessel became nevelah.`,
    '5#ג': `Its remedy. Shach wrote: but piercing the vessel from below or pouring from another side does not help — we fear perhaps the gravy will touch the wall forbidden through shaking; and it deals when there is not sixty in the food against that empty place — sixty times sixty-one slightly less against the drop, for the drop is forbidden until sixty; therefore with the drop there is sixty-one slightly less. It appears nevertheless one may not pour the food through that place of the pot, for one may not nullify issur l'chatchila; and even in a new pot it appears forbidden to pour lest perhaps it only steams in part of the vessel and was not nullified at first and now when pouring it will be nullified. What the Rav wrote in the gloss that in a new pot in every case one needs only sixty against the drop — meaning if he poured b'dieved. (Peri Chadash wrote: however if this vessel where the drop fell is not normally used for a small amount but only in abundance, it is permitted to use it, for impossible to reach noten ta'am as below siman 99 and 122.)`,
    '5#ד': `Ladle. Shach wrote: and even though it appears there is no distinction for the ladle — since it is new and he stirred afterward in the pot he should need only against what it absorbed — but here the pot is considered like old since they now cook meat in it and milk fell on it during cooking — nevertheless since you come to forbid the pot wall due to the drop, one must say perhaps cooking does not steam through the whole vessel and then the food above did not steam and if it steamed above the drop also spreads below; or say it deals with a new pot where the drop fell before the food was hot, or after the drop fell on a pot of water they put meat in it — requires study. (Peri Chadash wrote: know all this deals with an uncovered pot; but if covered at the time of falling we do not distinguish between opposite food and empty place — in every case sixty against the drop suffices either way — if the drop does not steam inward it is not forbidden, and if it steams inward since the pot is covered everything rises from the bottom to the mouth and mixes the drop in the whole pot and nullifies in sixty — see there.) Per the Rav's gloss siman 98 seif 5 one might be stringent to say even a new earthenware vessel chatichah na'asah nevelah — therefore here specifically for a drop of milk we say thus because all is still permitted; not for a drop of issur — but it is already explained siman 98 s.k. 20 that we cannot say the earthenware body becomes nevelah, and therefore here even for a drop of issur the law is thus; but in an old pot what is absorbed in it is chatichah na'asah nevelah — specifically an old pot beit yomo, otherwise what is in the pot walls does not become nevelah since noten ta'am li'fegam, as below siman 98.`,
    '6#א': `The gravy. Meaning even leaving until it cools does not help. Shach wrote: Maharai who was last and great in his generation investigated all customs and wrote they practice to permit through cooling as in Semak, and so Maharshal; however even per this custom it appears specifically in an old pot, but in a new pot certainly if there is sixty in the food against the drop they did not practice to forbid; and likewise in an old pot that is not beit yomo, or even beit yomo with sixty times sixty-one slightly less against the drop — in all this they did not practice issur and it is permitted for all, nevertheless wait until it cools.`,
    '6#ב': `The pot. And in Terumat HaDeshen he wrote one should be stringent l'chatchila to forbid the pot on erev Shabbat.`,
    '6#ג': `There is. We fear perhaps it did not steam all inward but something remained in the pot wall; and if perhaps it remained in the pot wall and spread in width until there is not sixty in the wall against it — then need sixty in the food against that wall that was forbidden; if it spread in width it spread in all until sixty, and if it did not spread in the whole vessel presumably it entered inward and was nullified — therefore the food is permitted but in the pot we fear perhaps a little remained in thickness that did not enter inward and it is forbidden; therefore if he cooked another dish in it its law is like the first time and sixty suffices if it fell opposite the gravy like the first time — Shach.`,
    '6#ד': `And he should pour. And not leave it to cool — thus after it fell opposite the gravy we fear lest it steam more from here onward (Orach Chaim).`,
    '7#א': `Sixty. Meaning sixty in the food against the drop alone. Maharshal wrote nevertheless the pot is forbidden. Shach wrote: leave it to cool if possible; but if needed immediately for guests etc. need not wait until cool but pour on another side; and same for any great need, great loss, or poor person per the teacher's judgment. Responsum R' Moshe Minz siman 15 wrote the custom is to tell them the reason why he is lenient so they are not astonished why sometimes he forbids and sometimes permits. Taz wrote in name of Orach Chaim: if a drop of milk fell on the pot with water by the fire and within me'eit l'eit they cooked meat in it — even if there was not sixty in the water against the drop the meat is permitted, for if it did not pass first into the pot also the second time it will not pass, and the meat is permitted b'dieved; and even if it passes inward first, there is only milk in the pot according to the estimated amount in the water, for in permitted we do not consider the whole piece as absorption itself and against that small matter presumably there is sixty in the meat. (I found in Torat HaEsh: if a drop fell on the vessel's ear the food is not forbidden.)`,
    '7#ב': `Hot. Excluding cold, for then even the pot is permitted.`,
    '7#ג': `Prevails. Taz wrote: this deals specifically if spilled on the stove place that is not hot from the fire and yad soledes bo does not apply there; but if spilled on the stove by the fire and it is hot yad soledes bo there, the milk in it is like kli rishon and needs sixty from what we estimate of milk in appearance under the pot. Shach wrote: it appears from Maharshal's words that if the pot boils from the fire's heat even the spill is forbidden even the food if there is not sixty against what is beneath it. (Specifically when there is no ash where the milk spilled, for if there is ash the spilled milk on the ash becomes for detriment and does not forbid as below siman 95 seif 21 — Mahari Italia.)`,
    '7#ד': `Like iruy. Meaning we do not say it cooled because it flowed on the stove; however this is specifically when yad soledes bo (Peri Chadash wrote per siman 98 we do not distinguish whether the stream broke — any heat that is yad soledes bo forbids — see there).`,
    '7#ה': `Kelipah. Shach wrote: therefore even if there is food in the pot where kelipah remains one need not peel, for the pot is like kelipah.`,
    '7#ו': `For the lower. Shach wrote: and no distinction to what is written siman 105 seif 3 in the gloss that if permitted and issur rest together and one is cold, kelipah suffices — here since the stream comes from above it is like the upper; or since the pot stands in place it is considered lower; or here it splashes on the pot as Bach end siman 91. (Peri Chadash wrote: specifically when it splashes on the pot; but if milk dripped under the meat pot the milk has the law of lower and does not forbid the food in the pot — but per Rashal and Shach I wrote below that if the pot boils from the fire even cold spill forbids the food until sixty — see there; therefore even if milk dripped under the pot it also forbids the food; and per Taz's words if the stove place is hot yad soledes bo the milk is like kli rishon and forbids the food too — requires study.) Or it deals here that it splashes on the pot; and Taz wrote to forbid where there is no great loss even if it does not splash on the pot, for we hold even in salting we forbid where there is no great loss when they touch one another — all the more here with heat through fire.`,
    '7#ז': `To boil. Terumat HaDeshen wrote: it appears if it did not begin to boil and fell on the cover, what is in the pot is not forbidden at all even without sixty, for it is still like cold and does not steam into what is in the pot. Taz wrote: it appears regarding the cover itself it is considered boiling since it rests on the pot by the fire and is forbidden. Maharshal disagrees with Rama and wrote: although boiling rises to the cover, the gravy does not join with it — if there is not sixty on the cover against the issur that fell on it the cover becomes nevelah. It appears to me the pot opposite the gravy differs because the gravy itself is always there, not so the cover — perhaps at the time the drop fell boiling did not reach the cover itself, and a Torah doubt is stringent; only in rabbinic issur one may be lenient.`,
    '8#_': `Touching. Taz wrote: for this reason it appears to permit if they baked in an oven of panada of milk and afterward in that place boiling they placed a pot with meat — as long as there is no milk in appearance in that place in the oven it is like a milk pot and two pots do not forbid one another.`,
    '9#_': `Of yours. Shach wrote: meaning a candle called kreuzel that burns from rendered-fat milk and is boiling from the fire; but milk of yours boiling in a pot that the pot boiled by the fire, scraping suffices, for it is not considered iruy since the stream broke as above seif 7 and siman 98 seif 10 in the gloss. Taz wrote: milk of yours — meaning from its mouth where the wick burns that side is considered through the fire itself; but if dripped from another side of the vessel where the wick is inside it is not called boiling to forbid what drips on it. Greater still Maharam wrote: if a drop of boiling milk fell on a knife or other cold vessel, only scraping where the drop fell suffices since the drop ceased at falling and did not come in a stream; specifically when falling on wood, but if on iron stab ten times in the ground and enough.`,
  },
  kereti: {
    '1#א': `The gentile tastes — see below siman 91.`,
    '1#ב': `And that piece is forbidden — see Peleti: reason it is forbidden if the gentile tastes and there is no milk taste — see there.`,
    '1#ג': `The cauldron — for then it absorbs and discharges only to discharge; we fear its taste, but if it rested then meat absorbed milk and chatichah na'asah nevelah and discharged again — gentile tasting does not help, for min b'mino, and needs sixty; why sixty suffices and not sixty-one, and why we do not fear repeated absorb-discharge — see Peleti; also his questions from other places and pot that cooked meat — see Peleti and all will be explained in its place.`,
    '2#א': `It is forbidden — see Peleti: that piece is forbidden because there is some fatness and pot steam also joins to spread in that piece, not the whole pot.`,
    '2#ב': `Only at the end — therefore at first that piece becomes nevelah and when stirring at the end it forbids the whole pot and needs sixty in the pot against the piece.`,
    '2#ג': `Until end of conclusion — for Talmudic language: if he stirred from the start he need not stir at the end; same with covering — Perishat Rashi.`,
    '2#ד': `And if he did not stir — if the piece is partly in the gravy and milk fell not opposite the gravy: for R' Yehuda as if in the gravy; for Rashi as if outside the gravy. Taz's view: that piece is forbidden and the rest of the pot does not join with it to nullify the milk in sixty; since it became nevelah it forbids the pot until sixty against the whole piece. Shach's view: it does not forbid the whole pot, only the piece, and all the more it does not join with the piece to nullify; but nevertheless need sixty in the pot against the milk that fell, for we say at falling it immediately spreads through the whole pot — see Peleti.`,
    '2#ה': `If there is sixty, etc. Shach explains the piece is half in the gravy; need sixty in the pot per R' Yehuda who spreads through the whole pot, only does not join to nullify per Rashi — see Peleti at length.`,
    '2#ו': `And not known, etc. See Peleti: Shach's view that since unknown we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah; I wrote it is not clear in Torah doubt in basar b'chalav; also that unknown means doubt whether it fell in the pot and became nevelah or did not and did not — proof from Talmud we do not establish prohibition; also nullified immediately at moment of falling before absorption — returns issur to permitted.`,
    '3#_': `Basar b'chalav — it is issur from itself and chatichah hefker.`,
    '4#א': `Except basar b'chalav — each alone is permitted and together forbidden.`,
    '4#ב': `Returns to permitted — see Peleti: no contradiction to what Mechaber wrote below siman 100 — see there.`,
    '4#ג': `Fell on it issur and stirred afterward — otherwise how would the pot be forbidden.`,
    '4#ד': `If we were like R' Yehuda's view as if wholly in the gravy and spreads in all the gravy.`,
    '4#ה': `And part of the piece — they are stringent like Rashi's view as if not opposite the gravy.`,
    '4#ו': `If wet mixed with wet — beliah does not apply; see Peleti so there is no contradiction to Tosafot in several places and what I wrote whether chatichah na'asah nevelah in other issurim is Torah or rabbinic depends on dispute of R' Tam and R' Chaim Cohen in reason as primary — see there.`,
    '4#ז': `Dry with dry — meaning not through cooking.`,
    '4#ח': `In any issur and salting — it appears several times where there is no great loss we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah in basar b'chalav; this is all per R' Efraim who does not say chatichah na'asah nevelah in other issurim; but for us we say because of ta'ama d'basar b'chalav; likewise rabbinic issur — see Peleti chametz on Pesach forbidden in mashahu — if it falls afterward in mashahu to the pot whether it forbids — see there.`,
    '5#א': `Not needed — if it steams and moves from its place it would steam into gravy and food; nevertheless the pot is forbidden — when the vessel is normally used only for a small amount, otherwise if used for abundance always nullifies.`,
    '5#ב': `In the place, etc. — we fear perhaps it steams until noten ta'am of the drop and chatichah na'asah nevelah and need sixty times sixty.`,
    '5#ג': `Do not touch — but do not pierce the pot below lest gravy flow — through shaking gravy rises and touches.`,
    '5#ד': `But new — even though now boiling rises we say either way: if boiling rises to place of drop absorption, the drop also descends into the gravy; if it cannot rise, the drop place cannot spread — same for old not beit yomo as new.`,
    '5#ה': `Old — and if the pot was covered at falling, sixty against the drop suffices.`,
    '6#א': `People practice — old pot: fear later boiling reached forbidden wall; new or not beit yomo or sixty times sixty-one slightly less — they did not practice issur — see Peleti.`,
    '6#ב': `Not opposite gravy — wait until cool if possible; if not, pour on another side — Acharonim.`,
    '7#א': `Not by the fire — if by the fire the lower prevails.`,
    '7#ב': `Stream broke — Peleti distinguishes liquid from solid — see siman 105.`,
    '7#ג': `Even, etc. — pot stands in place and this drips on it — it is iruy.`,
    '7#ד': `Its law, etc. — Maharshal disagrees; Taz: perhaps at that moment not so much boiling — Peleti: no need to be stringent.`,
    '7#ה': `And it, etc. — but if it did not begin to boil — judged like anything cold.`,
    '8#_': `Need sixty — I lean not to require sixty times sixty; but for one who is stringent sixty suffices.`,
    '9#_': `Wax candle — specifically when stream broke, heat not great; doctors' milk candles hotter — be stringent; if candle itself extinguished there — as if stream did not break — be stringent; but our candles where milk stands many days and is very hardened before becoming candles — gives taste for detriment — may be lenient.`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `Milk fell, etc. Shach s.k. 3 in name of Ran: a piece standing in the pot — from pot steam and moisture milk spreads in all of it even outside the gravy; Shach wrote on this no contradiction to siman 105 seif 4 thick vs. thin issur — see responsum Noda BiYehuda 2 YD end siman 25: when issur is actually attached to the piece and cooked with it, pot steam and moisture cause spread in all — as Tosafot Chullin 96 — see there.`,
    '2#_': `Wet permitted. Ba'er Heitev s.k. 13 in name of Maharshal Piskei Hagahot siman 60: specifically when mixed not through cooking; if through cooking we say chatichah na'asah nevelah — see responsum Noda BiYehuda 2 YD siman 49 on eight hides of sheep whose fat was rendered and mixed into five measures sheep fat and all rendered together then put into fifteen measures goose fat — permitted in great loss though Maharshal forbids if through cooking; from Rama's words it does not appear so; from Shach s.k. 15 it appears Shach leans lenient in wet-wet for great need — see siman 56 that Shach leans lenient in wet-wet in basar b'chalav for great need; perhaps Maharshal agrees in great loss; in any case one may rely on Rama especially only doubt of treifot — see siman 50 there.`,
    '3#_': `Then the pot is forbidden. Wherever pot forbidden is mentioned — if metal and comes to hag'alah, filling with water and boiling does not help the outer side — must insert whole pot in cauldron — see Magen Avraham OC siman 452 s.k. 11.`,
    '4#_': `For the lower prevails. [Ba'er Heitev wrote: specifically no ash, etc. — see Levushei Serad osef 135: some poskim erred permitting when milk spilled on stove with ash — mistake from Ba'er Heitev citing Mahari Italia; Mahari Italia wrote thus in general 106 but in general 107 s.k. 26 retracted b'tz'ar; therefore do not rely on ash except rabbinic issur like pouring boiling water from milk pot on wet meat pot — Shach siman 95 s.k. 5 and 17 may permit in great loss — not other cases.]`,
    '5#_': `To boil. Ba'er Heitev wrote Maharshal disagrees — see responsum Beit Yaakov siman 57 disagreeing with Maharshal and Taz and agreeing with Rama and Peri Chadash — see Pischei Teshuvah.`,
    '6#_': `Steam rises and is absorbed. See Pischei Teshuvah OC customs Orach Chaim order 2 osef 37: Rambam ch. 7 laws of food impurity law 4 — only steam of liquids is like them, but foods like rendered-fat milk — steam is not like them; therefore if milk pan below hot and kosher item above close — even yad soledes bo in steam perhaps permitted — see there; see responsum Bach Chadashot siman 24.`,
    '7#_': `Of milk. Ba'er Heitev of Mahari"t: may one light tobacco pipe from milk candle — see Bnei Chayyim siman 105 osef 4 brought proof forbidden by law not mere piety — see Hagahot there he retracted — see responsum Shabbetai Kohen: proof from Rashi Shabbat 22b lighting candle from candle — pipe absorbs oil and "it appears" refers to light; see YD 274 Taz and Nekudat HaKesef on Rashi; leniency if only detriment in milk itself; but l'chatchila lighting pipe from milk candle — detriment l'chatchila forbidden as several places; see Taz siman 47. Responsum Ma'aseh Moshe 4: one who is not careful may not light from milk candle for one who is careful — see.`,
    '8#_': `But milk. If wick fell from candle onto snuffbox and extinguished — responsum Beit Efrayim CM siman 17: I rule to be lenient and stringent: if head of wick already burned to ash it does not forbid; what is not yet ash certainly forbids; if rested on vessel until yad soledes bo it is absorbed through fire and needs libun; if not rested — hag'alah or scraping; here kli rishon does not apply since wick is moist in milk; Maharatz Beit Tzalel in gloss.`,
  },
  'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd': {
    '1#_': `(siman 92 Shach s.k. 3) Since we always estimate at sixty. It appears to me: nevertheless when the whole pot has sixty against the drop, that piece should also be permitted through netilah if it is fatty everything joins to nullify; if lean, netilah suffices even if sixty in the piece — by law only netilah not sixty; therefore still need pot-steam reasons that thin issur spreads in whole piece — see Peleti.`,
    '2#_': `(there s.k. 4) Except Rashba, etc. — Tevu'at Shor (99b) and so Shach s.k. 8; also from Rashba responsum on egg on coals in Beit Yosef above siman 66 — see Shach there he emends "above the egg."`,
    '3#_': `(there s.k. 6) One might say when no sixty in pot. Per Rama below he forbids all pieces for we cannot distinguish lean from fatty — if no sixty in whole pot everything is forbidden.`,
    '4#_': `(seif 2 gloss) Stir the whole pot. Why permitted to pour thus — forbidden to cook basar b'chalav; even if kafila says no taste perhaps truthfully there is taste and violated cooking in basar b'chalav — b'tz'ar.`,
    '5#_': `(Shach s.k. 8) And there is no nullifying issur. Still hard: if waited a bit, why not have gentile taste each piece lest this is the piece with taste — b'tz'ar.`,
    '6#_': `(Taz s.k. 6) Perhaps taste that piece. Why need this — without stirring must taste each piece; stirring mixes everything in doubt — if taste one piece and no taste, that piece is known permitted and rest is one in one — b'tz'ar.`,
    '7#_': `(Ra'avad) Then no need to stir. Hard: if waited, taste may perceive in one piece and forbid all before eating — b'tz'ar.`,
    '8#_': `(Shach s.k. 12) Only rabbinic. From Tosafot (100) proof for R' Tam that in large cauldron hag'alah chatichah na'asah nevelah is Torah — see.`,
    '9#_': `(seif 4 gloss) And all this in other issurim but basar b'chalav. Even basar b'chalav rabbinic — Shach siman 87 s.k. 3 on Rama seif 4 gloss.`,
  },
  'nekudot-hakesef': {
    '1#_': `(siman 92 Taz s.k. 2) It appears to me he holds like Rashi, etc. This is not proven immediately; one may say it deals when it is wholly outside the gravy and that which forbids the rest is when he stirred afterward as the end concludes — and so Bach, for Rambam holds like R' Yehuda; I brought this in Shach s.k. 8; and so Shach s.k. 4 that the view of all authors and Acharonim is like R' Yehuda — as I brought in Semag negative commandment 141 daf 51a and Semak siman 213 daf 101a and Mordechai and Agudah ch. Kol HaBasar and Ra'ah in Bedek HaBayit daf 99; and Maharil in laws of basar b'chalav and Orach Chaim general rule 25 and other authors all adopted R' Yehuda's reasoning.`,
    '2#_': `(there s.k. 4) His words are not clear; see in Shach.`,
    '3#_': `(there s.k. 5) Requires study, etc. And in Shach s.k. 6 I resolved this — see there. And that which he wrote and so it appears from Rama's words at the end of seif 4 that he rules like R' Yehuda — this is not clear; it will be understood from what I wrote in note 14 it does not exist, and everything was already explained properly in Shach s.k. 6 and s.k. 12 — see there.`,
    '4#_': `(there s.k. 16) His words are not correct in several different laws. And also in what he challenged Rama and Darkei Moshe and Bach he raised contradictions as I will explain. First what he meant regarding chametz on Pesach that was cooked with a piece and afterward that piece was cooked with others — it does not forbid in mashahu — is astonishing; I asked several elder teaching rabbis and great poskim and all said with one mouth they never practiced to forbid all mixtures. And so it appears from Tur Orach Chaim siman 447 and from Maharam's words there and as I wrote shortly. And so it appears in truth that nevertheless that mashahu taste in the first piece forbids the others again since chametz on Pesach forbids in mashahu whether in its species or not. And it is not comparable to what Tosafot and Rosh and Tur wrote regarding min b'mino for R' Yehuda that if the piece was first forbidden in mashahu due to its species and was cooked afterward with other pieces it does not forbid in mashahu even though they are its species — for there the reason is since we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah because it was not forbidden in noten ta'am, therefore one cannot say that mashahu in the pieces forbids all the other pieces since the piece did not become nevelah, therefore that mashahu relative to the other pieces is not its species — for we do not hear R' Yehuda who says min b'mino is not nullified except when that species is issur by itself, but not when another force is mixed in; and likewise here since the piece did not become nevelah, therefore that mashahu is mixed with another force and is considered not its species relative to the other pieces — for that mashahu is mixed and the pieces are not mixed if this species is not like its fellow. And so it appears explicitly from what Ran wrote end of ch. Gid HaNasheh and this is his language: and there is to infer here since Rav holds like R' Yehuda, why do I need that it impart taste in the piece — for even in mashahu it is forbidden since min b'mino is not nullified, etc.; and it appears to me that whenever it did not impart taste in the piece, even though it was forbidden, it is not considered issur to say the piece itself became nevelah; and whenever it did not become nevelah it does not forbid all the pieces, for even though R' Yehuda says min b'mino is not nullified, we do not hear thus whenever another taste of heter is mixed in — end of his words. But in chametz on Pesach in a cooked dish, since it forbids in mashahu whether in its species or not in its species, therefore even if you say that since another force of heter is mixed in it is considered not its species, nevertheless it forbids in mashahu even not in its species: it turns out Darkei Moshe and Beit Chadash intended well that in chametz on Pesach that piece forbids the others forever afterward. And perforce one must explain Rama's words on chametz on Pesach that he wrote here — it is as Beit Chadash wrote that the piece was mixed after Pesach, or it deals when mixed in dry, or it deals when mixed on erev Pesach after midday as I will write shortly: and proof for my words — for Tur Orach Chaim siman 467 wrote that wheat found in a pot forbids the spoon with which they stirred the pot and all other pots — end of his words: and further proof from what Maharam wrote there in the gloss seif 12 regarding wheat in a chicken that if mixtures were mixed and there is great loss and prevention of Yom Tov joy one may be lenient in all this in rabbinic issur doubts that are mashahu and one may permit other dishes that have mixture from that dish of meat that fell in water or was salted — end of his words; it implies certainly rabbinic issur such as a whole wheat found certainly on meat and the like — one must forbid the second mixture too even in great loss even though the meat was forbidden only due to mashahu: however one may challenge Darkei Moshe for another reason (as I challenged in Sefat Efrayim below siman 134 small seif 16 and in Sefat Efrayim siman 7 s.k. 14 I showed a place to study — see there) per what appears from his words that even in min b'mino for R' Yehuda we do not say that the piece forbidden in mashahu does not forbid the other pieces except when it fell to what is not its species, but when it returned and fell to its species then that mashahu also forbids the other pieces — and this is not so as explained in Tosafot and Rosh and Semak explicitly. And in this he properly challenged Taz. It turns out that in challenging Darkei Moshe his words are correct on one side but not on chametz on Pesach he did not challenge well, and regarding min b'mino he challenged well; and so I also challenged in Shach below siman 134 s.k. 16 on Darkei Moshe regarding min b'mino from words of Sefer HaTerumah and Tosafot and Rosh cited and from Orach Chaim — see there; and so in Rashba's novellae end of ch. Gid HaNasheh and I brought his language below; and so explained explicitly from Ran's words I brought shortly that regarding min b'mino even if it returned and fell to its species it does not forbid — and it emerges for a matter that has permitters where we hold taste is not nullified in its species where the piece was forbidden in mashahu due to davar she'yesh lo matirim as Shach siman 102 small seif 9 wrote — davar she'yesh lo matirim even its taste is not nullified in its species and it returned and fell to another pot even its species the other pot is permitted; and so emerges for chametz on Pesach in a dry matter per what we hold dry in dry on Pesach is not nullified due to davar she'yesh lo matirim such as when the piece was forbidden due to mashahu and that piece was mixed in others even in its species we do not say the others are forbidden due to davar she'yesh lo matirim — it is nullified not in its species and the piece forbidden due to mashahu did not become nevelah, therefore that mashahu in the first piece does not forbid the other pieces due to davar she'yesh lo matirim as written above. Or it emerges for chametz on erev Pesach after six hours per what we hold chametz is davar she'yesh lo matirim, therefore it is not nullified after six in its species even in a thousand only not in its species nullified at sixty as Shach siman 102 small seif 12 per Rambam's view — see there; and if so when the piece was forbidden on erev Pesach after six in mashahu due to its species because davar she'yesh lo matirim and was cooked afterward on erev Pesach even with its species it does not forbid for it did not become nevelah and also the first mashahu does not forbid since another force of heter is mixed in — if so it is considered not its species (nullified b'rov at six hours) — and this I wrote in Shach siman 134 small seif 16 such as a piece and the like forbidden in mashahu due to chametz on Pesach or davar she'yesh lo matirim and afterward fell to a pot, etc.; and my intent is similar to davar she'yesh lo matirim that it does not forbid in mashahu except in its species as I wrote shortly — and then even if it fell afterward to its species the law is thus and not like Darkei Moshe as I challenged there — see there: further I saw confusion in Taz's words that wrote: but we need to explain that which Tosafot wrote above in name of R' Tam that we do not say two mashahu — there it deals when the piece was forbidden in mashahu and returned and fell to another and they removed it — discharge does not forbid in mashahu as long as there is sixty against it; but in wet with wet, etc. — why do I need that they removed it to the piece, for even the piece there since it was forbidden only due to mashahu it does not forbid the other pieces as proven in Tosafot and Rosh and poskim explicitly that it deals only when they removed the issur itself, but the first piece forbidden due to mashahu remained there. And perhaps there is some scribal error in his words and he meant they removed to the issur and comes to say in wet with wet it does not help even when they removed to the issur — and thus the law appears correct that if something wet was forbidden due to mashahu it forbids afterward other mixtures in mashahu in its species only; but his plain language does not imply so. Also his later words are confused: also what he wrote afterward — and if we say it is nullified in the majority of the others this depends on the dispute Shulchan Aruch brought Orach Chaim siman 447 seif 9 and proper to be stringent — and so Levush there to be stringent in dry — and therefore what he ruled here that if one does not recognize the piece forbidden on Pesach it is nullified in majority is not correct at all and also contradicts his own words; rather Levush's words are correct and they do not contradict each other at all — for until now he did not rule Orach Chaim siman 446 seif 9 that it is not nullified except because it is davar she'yesh lo matirim and that is specifically in its species and specifically in chametz itself mixed in its species in dry or when the piece was forbidden in noten ta'am and returned and was mixed dry in its species — the law of davar she'yesh lo matirim that is not nullified in its species only; but here since it was forbidden due to mashahu, therefore we do not say it became nevelah and also we do not say that mashahu forbids afterward all the other pieces due to min b'mino since another force is mixed in — if so it is considered not its species relative to the others and everything is nullified in majority in dry if not recognized: further I saw an error in Taz's words — it appears from his words that R' Yehuda holds min b'mino is not nullified and so for us in chametz on Pesach or davar she'yesh lo matirim and the like where the piece was forbidden due to mashahu in its species and fell to another pot — it does not forbid in mashahu — for from this we need sixty against the piece that was forbidden. Also in Darkei Moshe it appears somewhat so. Nevertheless per my humble view it is a scribal error — for since we say it did not become nevelah because it was forbidden in mashahu, if so why would it need afterward sixty against the piece; and so proven in Tosafot and Rosh end of ch. Gid HaNasheh that it does not require sixty — see there regarding yayin nesech that fell to a pit and afterward a bucket of water fell; and so explicitly in Rosh last ch. Avodah Zarah: and that which it did not say the piece itself became nevelah and forbids all the pieces and water would be needed to nullify the wine — heter and wine issur — because forbidden wine did not fall in noten ta'am in the heter but in mashahu was forbidden, and every matter forbidden in mashahu we do not say chatichah na'asah nevelah as stated in ch. Gid HaNasheh, etc., until and now why did Rav need to say it imparted taste — for even without noten ta'am it also forbids the piece nevelah for the other pieces for R' Yehuda who says min b'mino in mashahu; rather certainly even though min b'mino was forbidden the mashahu from Torah we do not say the piece becomes nevelah and requires water to nullify all of it if it was not forbidden in noten ta'am — all the more yayin nesech for R' Yochanan who does not forbid in mashahu except rabbinically we do not require nullifying the heter too — end of his words; and so explicitly in Rashba's novellae end of ch. Gid HaNasheh; and so in Sefer HaTerumah daf 41a; and further in Sefer HaTerumah end of 42b; and so Orach Chaim general rule 24 law 3; and so Maharshal end of ch. Gid HaNasheh siman 58; and so I explained in Shach siman 134 small seif 16 — see there. I elaborated because several halachic novelties common among us emerge from this. The upshot: a piece forbidden due to davar she'yesh lo matirim and chametz after six hours and every matter that forbids in mashahu in its species — if that piece afterward fell to other pieces and was cooked with them even if also its species — it does not need sixty against the pieces and all the more it does not forbid in mashahu; and all the more when it fell to what is not its species it needs sixty only against the piece. And likewise a piece forbidden in mashahu due to chametz on Pesach and Pesach passed over it intentionally in a manner it remains in its issur — if it returned and was cooked with other pieces even in its species it does not need sixty against the piece; but chametz on Pesach itself that forbids in mashahu whether in its species or not forbids everything in mashahu; but if mixed in other pieces in dry even min b'mino it is nullified in majority even during Pesach; but if the piece was forbidden due to imparting taste and was mixed dry in its species even in a thousand it is not nullified due to davar she'yesh lo matirim; and if mixed not in its species in dry it is nullified at sixty — all this in a piece; but if something wet was forbidden due to mashahu and was cooked afterward that wet in its species — meaning the issur is the species of the second mixture — the second mixture is forbidden as if the first issur fell there; but if that wet was afterward mixed in what is not its species, the second mixture needs only sixty against the first issur — for we never say in a piece forbidden due to mashahu chatichah na'asah nevelah.`,
    '5#_': `(there Shach s.k. 27) Rather something remained in the pot wall. And one should not challenge perhaps something remained in the pot wall and spread in width until there is not sixty in the wall, etc. — it should be so.`,
    '6#_': `(there Taz s.k. 29) For this reason it appears to me to permit, etc. So too below siman 97 note 3 in a case that came before him — and see what he wrote there.`,
    '7#_': `(there s.k. 30) It appears to be stringent like Tosafot's view. And so I also raised in my book and also brought Hagahot Sh"D siman 49 in name of one who said every matter that begins an action came before Rashi, etc., that Rashi was in doubt whether we say immediately it becomes kli rishon or we say since he inserted the spoon in the pot and lifted with the spoon it is kli rishon — see there; and it implies there that the doubt leans to be stringent; and so briefly in Shach siman 107 s.k. 7; and he elaborated here with several weak proofs that are unnecessary.`,
    '8#_': `(there gloss in Beit Yosef) However what our teacher wrote that if there is not sixty in the milk even though he preceded and removed, also the milk is forbidden — is not intended in my view, etc. — see there; and in my humble opinion Tur's words are Rosh's words (siman 23): an olive-size of meat that fell into a cauldron of milk — meat forbidden and milk permitted; it deals when it fell into a boiling cauldron and he preceded and removed before it rested from boiling — what it absorbs it does not discharge; even though milk absorbs fatness discharged from the meat it has no measure to forbid it; but if there is not sixty in the milk against the meat and even if he removed immediately everything is forbidden — end; explained explicitly as Taz wrote; and so for Rambam and Rashba who require tasting for a gentile — we infer he holds that nevertheless it discharges of itself before removal — only since min b'eno mino he wrote kafila suffices; and so Ran and other poskim — see there; and requires study.`,
  },
};
